{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":5439,"output":2164,"cacheRead":15872,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":2073,"totalTokens":23475,"cost":{"input":0.000407925,"output":0.0005409999999999999,"cacheRead":0.00023807999999999997,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0011870049999999999}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Let me look at templates, static, and tests. Also check for specific issues I've spotted so far:\n\n1. **`main.go` silent inventory load failure**: when default `canopy.json` exists but `LoadInventory` fails, the error is silently swallowed (`if loaded, err := LoadInventory(\"canopy.json\"); err == nil`) — the app starts with zero config instead of reporting the misconfiguration. That contradicts the README/inventory philosophy (\"unknown fields are rejected so a misspelled setting cannot silently select a default\"). Concrete: main.go:37-41. Actually main.go lines: let me check exact lines.\n\n2. **`app.go` discoveryLoop hardcoded 5s ticker** — not configurable; also `refreshInterval` used for stale window; minor. Also `discoveryLoop` ignores error. Minor.\n\n3. **README HTTP surface**: README says Canopy serves only GET routes listed — matches. README says \"Trigger polling and Run outcomes remain separate signals\" — ok.\n\n4. **`view.go` auditView stale override**: `TrustClass = \"stale\"` when AuditedMaster != LastMaster even if result indicates failure — \"stale\" overrides \"error\". Hmm, README mentions commit \"pin audit divergence stale trust override\" — that's a pinned test. Not a finding.\n\n5. **`triggerViews`**: `view.AuditStatus = triggerStatus(trigger.StateKnown, trigger.AuditError)` — uses StateKnown for audit status rather than a dedicated audit-known flag. Possibly intentional.\n\n6. **`collector.go` `Logs` follow loop**: `if !timer.Stop() { <-timer.C }` — in the select after ctx.Done, `<-timer.C` could block if timer already fired and value consumed? Actually timer was just created; if ctx.Done fires, timer.Stop returns false only if timer already expired and value not drained. Since the loop just created it and 100ms interval, ctx.Done at same time... `<-timer.C` blocks until the timer fires (up to 100ms). But ctx already cancelled — blocking on timer.C would delay up to 100ms; fine. Actually wait, the select already chose ctx.Done, meaning timer.C not ready. timer.Stop() returns true in that case (timer not yet fired). OK.\n\n7. **`refreshLoop` timer.Reset after wake**: after handling wake or timer, it stops and resets timer — that means every refresh resets interval; fine.\n\n8. **`server.go` sanitizeLogText**: redaction patterns — `keyPattern` requires 12+ chars; `bearerPattern` sk-. \"Keep credentials out\" — the README says Run logs can contain sensitive output; sanitization exists. Not a defect necessarily.\n\n9. **`handleLogs` passes `follow=false`** but collector.Logs supports follow param; the `follow` param and `logsPollInterval` loop are only exercised... is follow ever true? grep for \"true\" passed to Logs. In server.go: `a.collector.Logs(ctx, instance, runID, false)`. So follow path is dead code in production — unused exported surface/dead weight? The `follow` parameter is never used with true anywhere. Also HTMX polling paused when hidden (commit). This is a candidate finding: dead parameter / untested path. Check tests.\n\n10. **discovery.go scanSystemdUnits**: `strings.Contains(workDir, \"/misty-step/\")` — hardcoded boundary string, and requires workDir to contain \"/misty-step/\" — a workdir exactly ending in \"/misty-step\" wouldn't match. Also hardcoded \"/misty-step/\" is Misty-Step specific; README describes Canopy generically. Architecture drift: Canopy README describes a general operator view but discovery is hard-coded to the \"Misty Step boundary\" — README doesn't mention auto-discovery at all! README's HTTP surface and inventory section never mention auto-discovery. That's a stale-docs finding: README says \"Inventory\" with explicit instances but code auto-discovers and merges. Actually README boundary section: \"Canopy treats every Forest as an external service.\" Discovery reads systemd units and the filesystem — that's reading host state, not `.forest` data, fine. But README omission: main.go auto-discovery is not documented. Stale docs contradict shipped behavior — candidate.\n\n11. **discovery ID collision**: `sanitizeID(entry.Name())` — two different dirs could sanitize to same ID (\"my_repo\" and \"my-repo\" both → \"my-repo\"). candidates map is keyed by root path, so duplicates with same ID but different roots can both enter `instances`. Then syncDiscoveredInstances: discMap keyed by ID, so one overwrites; but the other instance silently dropped. Minor. Actually in `DiscoverLocalInstances`, instances list may contain two Instances with the same ID and different Roots. syncDiscoveredInstances discMap keyed by ID dedupes silently. Not a big deal.\n\n12. **`validateInstance` for local instances**: root/forest only checked for NUL; no existence check — fine.\n\n13. **`main.go`**: `discovered, err := DiscoverLocalInstances(ctx)` — err always nil actually (function never returns error). Minor.\n\n14. **`app.go` discoveryLoop** every 5s runs `systemctl` + `os.ReadDir` — hardcoded interval, ignoring inventory config. Also each loop calls systemctl with 2s timeout; fine.\n\n15. **`server.go` handleFleet**: calls `a.selectInstance(id)` when id provided but doesn't send HX-Trigger-After-Swap like handleInstance — inconsistent? Minor.\n\n16. **view.go `pageView`**: stale window is 3× refreshInterval computed from current selected status at render time; fine.\n\n17. **collector timeout vs refreshTimeout**: refreshTimeout 30s in app, collector timeout 30s default; Collect runs multiple commands sequentially (version, config, declaration list + N shows, status) — with 30s total context and per-command 30s timeout... Each command gets `context.WithTimeout(ctx, c.timeout)` where ctx is the app's 30s-limited parent. So overall bounded. Fine.\n\n18. **`newCLICollector` unexported wrapper** — check if used only in tests. grep.\n\n19. **`handlePage` renders full page for non-HTMX**; fine.\n\n20. **`runParam`**: if multiple values (len>1) returns errMissingRun? `if !present || len(values)==0 || (len(values)==1 && values[0]==\"\")` → missing; else bad. len 2 with non-empty → errBadRun. ok.\n\n21. **security**: `/fragments/fleet` with instance param calls selectInstance — GET mutating selection; fine for read-only.\n\n22. **templates**: check templates and static quickly. Also tests coverage: server_test, app_test etc. Check whether `follow` path and `discoveryLoop` are tested.\n\n23. **README says HTMX 2.0.9 vendored** — check static dir.\n\n24. **model.go TriggerData.Stale comment** etc fine.\n\nPotential findings (need concrete evidence + not style):\n\nA. main.go:37-40 — default canopy.json decode error silently ignored; app starts with zero instances and no diagnostics, contradicting inventory.go's stated rejection policy. Required: surface the load error (log.Fatalf or log warning). Strong.\n\nB. README.md inventory/HTTP sections omit auto-discovery behavior shipped in discovery.go/main.go (background merge, systemd scanning) — stale doc contradiction. Concrete README lines. Also README says \"Canopy treats every Forest as an external service\" — fine. The finding: README \"Inventory\" documents only explicit config, but main.go:60-64 and app.go:111-127 auto-discover and merge instances not present in inventory; operators reading README can't predict fleet contents. Required: document discovery. Good finding.\n\nC. collector.go Logs follow parameter dead in production — server.go:~119 passes false always; the follow loop (collector.go:~215-230) is unreachable in shipped behavior and likely untested... check tests first.\n\nD. discovery.go:~60 `strings.Contains(workDir, \"/misty-step/\")` — substring boundary check: a workdir at `/home/u/misty-step` (no trailing slash, exact end) fails; also any repo path containing \"/misty-step/\" anywhere (e.g. `/srv/misty-step-archive/x`) passes. Weak boundary. Also hardcoded product-specific constant in generic operator view. Hmm — but repo conventions: this is Misty Step's tool, so specific is fine. The substring issue though: workDir `/home/phaedrus/development/misty-step/canopy` contains it, fine. Edge case. Maybe weak; but \"contains\" vs proper path-prefix is a real correctness nit. Medium.\n\nE. discovery.go sanitizeID collision: two sibling dirs `agent_test` and `agent-test` both → id \"agent-test\"; DiscoverLocalInstances returns both (keyed by root), syncDiscoveredInstances discMap keeps one, other silently dropped with no error. Evidence: discovery.go:185-200 (sanitizeID) + discovery.go:38-44 (map keyed by root, IDs unverified for uniqueness). Required: dedupe by ID or make IDs unique. Medium.\n\nF. app.go discoveryLoop hardcoded 5s ticker ignoring inventory intervals; every 5s spawns systemctl — complecting/perf nit. Possibly style.\n\nG. server.go handleFleet sets selection without HX-Trigger-After-Swap unlike handleInstance (server.go:47-49 vs 74-77) — is that a bug? handleFleet renders full pageView including fleet; the trigger event \"canopy-selection\" is used by frontend JS to pause polling? Let me check static JS.\n\nLet me check static/main.js and templates to verify G, and tests for follow."}}
