{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_delta","contentIndex":0,"delta":"{\"command\":\"powder note canopy-critic-stale-selected-window --agent \\\"${POWDER_AGENT:-critic}\\\" --text 'filed-by: forest-misty-step/canopy @ misty-step/canopy\\ndeployment: canopy worktree 1788550542915580458-critic, git c203dc9118ace7575d8aab1162f11edc2e3b601a (running build revision unknown)\\nObserved: view.go:172 passes maxAge = 3*a.refreshInterval(instance.ID) to instanceView, and view.go:155-157 classifies an instance Stale when now >= LastSuccess+maxAge even with state.Err == nil. For the selected instance, refreshInterval is SelectedIntervalSeconds (default 2s, inventory.go:27), so the staleness window is 6s. A successful Collect (collector.go:131-208) runs 4+N subprocesses (version, config show, declaration list, one declaration show per declaration, status), each individually bounded by a 30s timeout (collector.go:17, app.go:16), so a healthy selected instance — especially over SSH — can routinely take longer than 6s per cycle. Result: templates/instance.html:16 and fleet.html:21 render the \\\"stale\\\" badge/state-dot for an instance whose collection is succeeding with no error, contradicting the README boundary that stale marks failed/missed refreshes, not healthy ones.\\nRequired: freshness must not report stale for an instance whose refreshes are succeeding; the missed-refresh window must exceed the worst-case collection time for that instance.\\nProposed spec direction: derive maxAge from the collection bound (e.g. refreshTimeout plus margin, or observed last cycle duration) instead of 3x the poll interval, or measure and store per-instance refresh cycle duration; pin with a test where a selected instance with a slow but successful collector renders fresh, not stale.' && powder note canopy-critic-discovery-id-collision --agent \\\"${POWDER_AGENT:-critic}\\\" --text 'filed-by: forest-misty-step/canopy @ misty-step/canopy\\ndeployment: canopy worktree 1788550542915580458-critic, git c203dc9118ace7575d8aab1162f11edc2e3b601a (running build revision unknown)\\nObserved: discovery.go:16 keys discovered candidates by root path, so DiscoverLocalInstances can return multiple instances whose sanitized IDs collide: sanitizeID (discovery.go:196-210) maps \\\"agent_test\\\" and \\\"agent-beta\\\" style names to the same ID (both \\\"agent-test\\\", underscores and dashes both normalize to \\\"-\\\"). app.go:239-241 then builds discMap keyed by instance ID, so the second colliding instance silently overwrites the first in the map and, at app.go:275-283, is skipped via existingMap — a working Forest checkout simply never appears in the fleet, with no log or error. The same silent-drop applies if a discovered ID matches an explicit inventory ID.\\nRequired: every discoverable, validated instance must either appear in the fleet or its omission must be surfaced; discovery IDs must be unique per instance or collisions must be resolved deterministically and observably.\\nProposed spec direction: make DiscoverLocalInstances deduplicate or disambiguate by ID (e.g. suffix the ID from the root path on collision) before returning, or make syncDiscoveredInstances log/record dropped collisions; add a discovery test with two sibling dirs whose names sanitize to the same ID.' && powder note canopy-critic-auditstatus-dead-surface --agent \\\"${POWDER_AGENT:-critic}\\\" --text 'filed-by: forest-misty-step/canopy @ misty-step/canopy\\ndeployment: canopy worktree 1788550542915580458-critic, git c203dc9118ace7575d8aab1162f11edc2e3b601a (running build revision unknown)\\nObserved: view.go:61 declares TriggerViewModel.AuditStatus and view.go:344 computes it via triggerStatus(trigger.StateKnown, trigger.AuditError), but no template renders it (grep over templates/*.html shows zero references to AuditStatus) and no test asserts it. The trigger matrix in instance.html renders only PollStatus, RunStatus, and ConsecutiveErrors; AuditError reaches the UI only through the aggregated Errors list (view.go:262-268). This is unused computed surface: it costs a maintenance obligation on every triggerStatus change while contributing nothing observable.\\nRequired: shipped view surface is either consumed by a rendered behavior or removed; per repository conventions the projection should not carry unrendered derived fields.\\nProposed spec direction: either delete TriggerViewModel.AuditStatus and its assignment, or render an Audit column in the trigger matrix if the operator surface is meant to show per-trigger audit state; note the semantics question that view.go:344 reuses StateKnown (poll state) for audit status, which needs its own known flag if rendered.'\"}"}}
